<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Suppressions for OWASP Dependency-Check.

  Dependency-Check identifies libraries by matching their metadata against CPE
  names in the NVD. That matching is name-based, so a package can be attributed
  a CVE belonging to an unrelated product with a similar name. Those are the
  false positives this file exists to record.

  The standard for adding one here is the same as for dismissing a CodeQL
  alert: a written reason a reviewer who has not read the code can evaluate,
  naming what was checked and why the CVE does not apply. "It is noisy" is not
  a reason. If a finding is real but accepted, say that explicitly and say who
  accepted it, rather than describing it as a false positive.

  Prefer the narrowest suppression that works. A <packageUrl> plus a single
  <cve> suppresses one finding for one package; a bare <cpe> can silence an
  entire product across future versions, including a genuine advisory published
  later.

  Format reference:
  https://dependency-check.github.io/DependencyCheck/general/suppression.html

  Example of the shape a real entry takes:

  <suppress>
    <notes><![CDATA[
      CVE-YYYY-NNNNN is against <other product>, which shares a CPE prefix with
      this package but is unrelated code by a different vendor. Verified by
      reading the advisory's affected-product list on <date>.
    ]]></notes>
    <packageUrl regex="true">^pkg:nuget/Some\.Package@.*$</packageUrl>
    <cve>CVE-YYYY-NNNNN</cve>
  </suppress>
-->
<suppressions xmlns="https://jeremylong.github.io/DependencyCheck/dependency-suppression.1.3.xsd">
  <!-- No suppressions yet. The scan is clean; add entries here only with a
       reason, and only when a finding has actually been investigated. -->
</suppressions>
